---
import DemonstrationCard from '../DemonstrationCard.astro';
import ExecutionDemo from '../ExecutionDemo.astro';
import ExecutionTiming from '../ExecutionTiming.astro';
---

<section id="setup" class="section setup-showcase">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <h2>02 Benchmark composition and evaluation protocol</h2>
    <div class="report-prose">
      <section
        id="evaluation-tasks"
        class="setup-subsection"
        aria-labelledby="evaluation-tasks-title"
      >
        <h3 id="evaluation-tasks-title">Evaluation tasks</h3>
        <p>
          The study comprises a main EBench evaluation and a separate, small-scale proof of concept
          (POC) on task composition.
        </p>
        <h4>EBench: generalization under controlled variations</h4>
        <p>
          The main evaluation covers the complete EBench test set, including{' '}
          <strong>26 tasks and 510 episodes</strong>, spanning nine scene categories across household, retail, and industrial
          settings. The tasks include <strong>19 mobile and 7 tabletop tasks</strong>, with
          annotations for mobility, manipulation precision, and task horizon. Controlled variations
          in objects, backgrounds, instructions, and their combinations probe generalization under
          changing conditions.
        </p>
        <h4>Beyond EBench: unseen task compositions</h4>
        <p>
          The compositional POC sits <strong>outside the 26-task main evaluation</strong>. It asks a
          robot to place a bookmark and then a pen on the same open book, combining the familiar
          <strong>Bookmark on Book</strong> task with <strong>Pen on Book</strong>, which is absent
          from robot-policy training. The{' '}
          <a href="#case-poc-setting">compositional evaluation setting</a> details the training-task
          coverage, prescribed sequence, and scoring protocol.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section
        id="adaptation-protocol"
        class="setup-subsection"
        aria-labelledby="adaptation-protocol-title"
      >
        <h3 id="adaptation-protocol-title">Model adaptation and evaluation protocol</h3>
        <h4>Main evaluation: learned policies and one-shot ICL</h4>
        <p>
          The VLA/WAM policies are trained on the publicly released EBench dataset, which contains{' '}
          <strong>6,600 episodes across 26 tasks</strong>. We compare Astra with seven learned
          policies using their reported EBench leaderboard results.
        </p>
        <p>
          GPT-6-Astra runs with <code class="reasoning-effort-highlight">high</code> reasoning
          effort and receives{' '}
          <strong>one annotated demonstration per task, without parameter updates</strong>. A
          separate GPT-6-Astra instance reviews one video trajectory from the training set,
          autonomously selects keyframes, and writes the accompanying prompts. Every episode of a
          task uses the same ICL package. Astra uses that demonstration as context for live
          decisions, rather than replaying its coordinates.
        </p>
        <DemonstrationCard />
        <p>
          Physical execution in the main evaluation follows the{' '}
          <strong>same task-specific physics-step limits as the baseline policies</strong>. We
          impose no additional aggregate cap on Astra's tokens, tool calls, or inference time. Astra
          may issue a termination signal before the execution limit; the server determines the final
          task success and Score. If the policy stops early, the runner holds the robot and advances
          the simulator to obtain that terminal result.
        </p>
        <p>
          Within each task, <strong>success rate (SR)</strong> is the fraction of successful
          episodes, and <strong>Score</strong> is the mean normalized terminal partial credit under
          that task's scoring rules. The main evaluation aggregates these task means with{' '}
          <strong>equal weight across all 26 tasks</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a comparison with reported leaderboard results, rather than an experiment in which
          all model conditions are held constant. The available records do not establish identical
          camera inputs, control interfaces, scene revisions, or inference budgets across models;
          observed performance differences therefore cannot be attributed to model capability alone.
        </p>
        <h4>Compositional POC: zero-shot execution</h4>
        <p>
          Comparison policies are retrained on the atomic-task data described above. No ground-truth
          trajectories are available for the composed task, and all models use{' '}
          <strong>zero-shot execution of the unseen combination</strong>; Astra receives no
          in-context demonstration for it. The POC reports success and partial-completion scores
          over <strong>10 rollouts per method</strong>, separately from the EBench results. These
          rollouts are not included in the main evaluation's 510 episodes or headline task averages.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section
        id="observations-control"
        class="setup-subsection"
        aria-labelledby="observations-control-title"
      >
        <h3 id="observations-control-title">Observations and direct robot control</h3>
        <h4>Inputs: camera views and robot state</h4>
        <p>
          GPT-6-Astra receives{' '}
          <strong>RGB images from the overview, left wrist, and right wrist cameras</strong>,
          together with end-effector poses, gripper state, base pose, and the current simulator
          timestep. All three views are supplied initially; subsequent tool calls return the camera
          views requested by the model.
        </p>
        <p>
          The model receives{' '}
          <strong>
            no depth data, ground-truth object poses, camera calibration, segmentation, intermediate
            task scores, or subgoal-completion signals
          </strong>
          . The timestep reports elapsed simulation steps; the task's maximum physics-step budget is
          not supplied to the model.
        </p>
        <h4>Outputs: action requests through a fixed interface</h4>
        <p>
          Astra requests{' '}
          <strong>end-effector (EEF) poses, gripper and base targets, and action durations</strong>.
          A fixed robot execution interface carries out those requests.{' '}
          <strong>No learned VLA or WAM generates or refines Astra's actions.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          The interaction follows an <strong>observation–decision–execution–feedback loop</strong>:
          Astra interprets the current images and state, chooses the next action request, and waits
          while the interface executes it.{' '}
          <strong>Updated observations arrive when the complete tool call returns</strong>, after
          its waypoint sequence has run. Those observations inform the next decision; the model does
          not receive a new decision point after each internal execution chunk.
        </p>

        <ExecutionTiming />
        <ExecutionDemo />
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .setup-subsection {
    margin-block: 2.75rem 0;
    text-align: left;
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--header-height) + 1.5rem);
  }
  .setup-subsection > h3 {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    font-size: 1.875rem;
  }
  .setup-subsection > h4 {
    margin: 1.75rem 0 0.875rem;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .reasoning-effort-highlight {
    background-color: #5b616b;
    color: #fff;
    padding: 0.1em 0.3em;
    border-radius: 0.2em;
  }
</style>
